// Mock PDF content generator for testing curriculum imports
export const mockPDFContent = {
  ontarioMathGrade4: `
Ontario Mathematics Curriculum
Grade 4

Overall Expectations

By the end of Grade 4, students will:

Number Sense and Numeration
B1. Demonstrate an understanding of numbers and make connections to the way numbers are used in everyday life
B2. Use knowledge of numbers and operations to solve mathematical problems encountered in everyday life

Specific Expectations

B1. Number Sense
B1.1 Read, represent, compose, and decompose whole numbers up to and including 10 000, using appropriate tools and strategies, and describe various ways they are used in everyday life
B1.2 Compare and order whole numbers up to and including 10 000, in various contexts
B1.3 Estimate the number of objects in collections of up to 10 000 and verify their estimates by counting
B1.4 Round whole numbers to the nearest ten, hundred, or thousand, in various contexts
B1.5 Describe various ways that numbers up to 10 000 are used in everyday life

B2. Operations
B2.1 Use the properties of operations, and the relationships between addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division, to solve problems involving whole numbers, including those requiring more than one operation, and check calculations
B2.2 Recall and demonstrate multiplication facts from 0 × 0 to 12 × 12, and related division facts
B2.3 Use mental math strategies to multiply whole numbers by 10, 100, and 1000, divide whole numbers by 10, and add and subtract decimal tenths
B2.4 Represent and solve problems involving the multiplication of two- or three-digit whole numbers by one-digit whole numbers
B2.5 Represent and solve problems involving the division of two- or three-digit whole numbers by one-digit whole numbers
B2.6 Represent and solve problems involving the addition and subtraction of whole numbers up to 10 000
B2.7 Demonstrate an understanding of fractions by representing, comparing, and ordering fractions with like and unlike denominators
B2.8 Demonstrate an understanding of decimal numbers in tenths

Measurement
C1. Demonstrate an understanding of the use and measurement of time, length, mass, and capacity

C1.1 Estimate, measure, and record length, perimeter, area, mass, and capacity, using a variety of strategies
C1.2 Solve problems involving the relationship between seconds and minutes, minutes and hours, hours and days, days and weeks, and weeks and years
C1.3 Identify benchmark angles, using a reference tool, and estimate angles
C1.4 Measure and construct angles up to 180° using a protractor

Geometry and Spatial Sense
E1. Describe and represent shape, location, and movement by applying geometric properties and spatial relationships

E1.1 Identify geometric properties of rectangles, including the number of right angles, parallel and perpendicular sides, and lines of symmetry
E1.2 Classify two-dimensional shapes by side and angle properties
E1.3 Identify and describe the relative locations of objects using positional language
E1.4 Give and follow multi-step instructions involving movement from one location to another

Data Management and Probability
D1. Manage, analyze, and use data to make convincing arguments and informed decisions

D1.1 Create surveys and collect data from different primary and secondary sources
D1.2 Collect data by conducting observations, surveys, and experiments
D1.3 Display data in stem-and-leaf plots and multiple-bar graphs
D1.4 Determine the mean and identify the mode(s), if any, for various data sets
D1.5 Analyze different sets of data presented in various ways
D1.6 Draw conclusions by comparing sets of data
`,

  frenchScienceGrade2: `
Programme de sciences et technologie
2e année

Contenus d'apprentissage

Systèmes vivants : Les animaux : croissance et changements

Attentes
À la fin de la 2e année, l'élève doit pouvoir :

SV1. Démontrer sa compréhension des caractéristiques et des besoins des animaux
SV2. Examiner les besoins et les caractéristiques des animaux et les façons dont ces caractéristiques répondent à leurs besoins
SV3. Démontrer sa compréhension que les animaux grandissent et changent

Contenus d'apprentissage spécifiques

SV1.1 Observer et comparer les caractéristiques physiques d'une variété d'animaux
SV1.2 Observer et décrire les changements dans l'apparence et l'activité des animaux à mesure qu'ils grandissent
SV1.3 Identifier les besoins constants des animaux pour leur survie
SV1.4 Comparer les façons dont différents animaux répondent à leurs besoins

SV2.1 Classer les animaux en utilisant diverses caractéristiques
SV2.2 Décrire les adaptations qui permettent aux animaux de satisfaire leurs besoins
SV2.3 Identifier les façons dont les animaux sont utiles aux humains
SV2.4 Comparer les façons dont les humains et les animaux utilisent leurs sens

SV3.1 Identifier et décrire les principaux stades du cycle de vie d'une variété d'animaux
SV3.2 Comparer les cycles de vie de différents animaux
SV3.3 Prédire comment la croissance et les changements pourraient affecter les animaux

Matière et énergie : Les propriétés des liquides et des solides

ME1. Démontrer sa compréhension des propriétés des liquides et des solides
ME2. Examiner les propriétés des liquides et des solides

ME1.1 Identifier les propriétés des liquides et des solides
ME1.2 Décrire les propriétés de l'eau sous ses trois états
ME1.3 Identifier les conditions qui causent les changements d'état
ME1.4 Décrire comment les liquides et les solides sont utilisés

ME2.1 Examiner les propriétés de divers liquides et solides
ME2.2 Examiner les façons de combiner des liquides et des solides
ME2.3 Déterminer les liquides qui se mélangent et ceux qui ne se mélangent pas
`,

  englishLanguageArtsGrade5: `
English Language Arts Curriculum
Grade 5

Strand A: Oral Communication

Overall Expectations
A1. Listening to Understand: listen in order to understand and respond appropriately in a variety of situations for a variety of purposes
A2. Speaking to Communicate: use speaking skills and strategies appropriately to communicate with different audiences for a variety of purposes
A3. Reflecting on Oral Communication Skills and Strategies: reflect on and identify their strengths as listeners and speakers, areas for improvement, and the strategies they found most helpful in oral communication situations

Specific Expectations

A1. Listening to Understand
A1.1 Purpose: identify purposes for listening in a variety of situations, formal and informal, and set goals related to specific listening tasks
A1.2 Active Listening Strategies: demonstrate an understanding of appropriate listening behaviour by adapting active listening strategies to suit a variety of situations, including work in groups
A1.3 Comprehension Strategies: identify a variety of listening comprehension strategies and use them appropriately before, during, and after listening in order to understand and clarify the meaning of oral texts
A1.4 Demonstrating Understanding: demonstrate an understanding of the information and ideas in a variety of oral texts by summarizing important ideas and citing important details
A1.5 Making Inferences: make inferences using stated and implied ideas in oral texts
A1.6 Extending Understanding: extend understanding of oral texts by connecting the ideas in them to their own knowledge, experience, and insights; to other texts, including print and visual texts; and to the world around them
A1.7 Analyzing Texts: analyze oral texts and explain how specific elements in them contribute to meaning
A1.8 Point of View: identify the point of view presented in oral texts and ask questions to identify missing or possible alternative points of view
A1.9 Presentation Strategies: identify the presentation strategies used in oral texts and analyze their effect on the audience

Strand B: Reading

Overall Expectations
B1. Reading for Meaning: read and demonstrate an understanding of a variety of literary, graphic, and informational texts, using a range of strategies to construct meaning
B2. Understanding Form and Style: recognize a variety of text forms, text features, and stylistic elements and demonstrate understanding of how they help communicate meaning
B3. Reading With Fluency: use knowledge of words and cueing systems to read fluently
B4. Reflecting on Reading Skills and Strategies: reflect on and identify their strengths as readers, areas for improvement, and the strategies they found most helpful before, during, and after reading

Specific Expectations

B1. Reading for Meaning
B1.1 Variety of Texts: read a variety of texts from diverse cultures, including literary texts, graphic texts, and informational texts
B1.2 Text Features: identify a variety of text features and explain how they help readers understand texts
B1.3 Comprehension Strategies: identify a variety of reading comprehension strategies and use them appropriately before, during, and after reading to understand texts
B1.4 Demonstrating Understanding: demonstrate understanding of a variety of texts by summarizing important ideas and citing supporting details
B1.5 Making Inferences: make inferences about texts using stated and implied ideas from the texts as evidence
B1.6 Extending Understanding: extend understanding of texts by connecting the ideas in them to their own knowledge, experience, and insights, to other familiar texts, and to the world around them
B1.7 Analyzing Texts: analyze texts and explain how various elements in them contribute to meaning
B1.8 Responding to and Evaluating Texts: make judgements and draw conclusions about the ideas and information in texts and cite stated or implied evidence from the text to support their views
B1.9 Point of View: identify the point of view presented in texts, ask questions to identify missing or alternative points of view, and suggest some possible alternative perspectives
`
};

